        "\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Regression predicts a number, like a house price or tomorrow's temperature. Classification predicts a category, like whether an email is spam or which type of mine an object is. So regression is judged by how close its guesses are to the real values, and classification is judged by how often it picks the right category.\n",
        "2. A confusion table lines up what the model predicted against what actually happened. The diagonal shows the predictions it got right, and everything else shows where it messed up. It's useful because you can see which classes the model handles well, which ones it mixes up, and whether it tends to give false positives or false negatives. That's important because some mistakes are a lot worse than others.\n",
        "3. SSE (sum of squared errors) tells you how far off a model's predictions are from the actual values. You take each error, square it, and add them all up. Lower SSE means a better fit. Squaring makes big misses count a lot more than small ones. It's best to check SSE on test data, since a more complex model will always lower SSE on training data even when it's just overfitting.\n",
        "4. Overfitting is when a model is too complex and basically memorizes the training data, including all its random noise. It looks great on the data it was trained on but does poorly on new data. Underfitting is the opposite. The model is too simple to pick up the real patterns, so it does poorly on both the training data and new data. With k-NN, a really small k (like k = 1) tends to overfit, and a really large k tends to underfit. The goal is to land somewhere in between.\n",
        "5. If you judge a model on the same data it learned from, it'll look better than it really is, and you'd end up rewarding overfitting. Splitting the data lets you train on one part and test on a part the model has never seen, which gives a more honest idea of how it'll do on new data. Trying different values of k and picking the one with the best test accuracy (or lowest test SSE) helps you find the sweet spot between overfitting and underfitting. One thing to be careful of though is that since the test set is being used to pick k, it's not totally \"unseen\" anymore, so the final score can be a little optimistic.\n",
        "6. A class label is simple and easy to act on. The model just says \"this is a mine\" or \"this isn't,\" which is great when you need a clear decision. The downside is that it hides how confident the model is, so a 51% call and a 99% call look exactly the same.A probability distribution gives the chances for each class, like 70% mine and 30% rock. That shows how confident the model is and lets you set your own cutoff depending on how costly different mistakes are. For example, you might flag anything over 20% as a possible mine to be safe. The downside is that it's harder to interpret, someone still has to turn it into a decision, and the probabilities aren't always well calibrated, so a \"70%\" might not really mean 70%."
      ],
